	<logo>https://static.globalissues.org/i/globalissues/logo-feed.jpg</logo><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41797</id><title>Why the UN Environment Assembly is Essential to a Safer, More Resilient Planet</title><updated>2025-12-03T08:47:52-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41797" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2025/12/unea.jpg" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2025/12/unea.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
NAIROBI, Kenya, December 3 (IPS)  - As geopolitical challenges and tensions escalate globally, one thing is clear: fragmented politics will not fix a fractured planet. This is why the United Nations Environment Assembly (UNEA) – the world’s highest decision-making body on the environment – is so critical to address our shared and emerging environmental threats.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41797&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Why the UN Environment Assembly is Essential to a Safer, More Resilient Planet”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2025/12/unea-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41807</id><title>General Assembly demands return of Ukrainian children by Russia</title><updated>2025-12-03T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41807" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/03-12-2025-UN-Photo-ESS-Ukraine.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/03-12-2025-UN-Photo-ESS-Ukraine.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The UN General Assembly on Wednesday demanded by a wide margin that Russia immediately and unconditionally return all Ukrainian children who have been forcibly transferred or deported since the start of the war.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41807&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “General Assembly demands return of Ukrainian children by Russia”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/03-12-2025-UN-Photo-ESS-Ukraine.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41806</id><title>Sudan’s crisis deepens with communities trapped in ‘siege conditions’</title><updated>2025-12-03T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41806" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/12-11-2025-UNICEF-Sudan-10.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/12-11-2025-UNICEF-Sudan-10.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Civilians across Sudan’s Kordofan region are facing extreme hardship as conflict intensifies, warned the UN aid coordination office (OCHA) on Wednesday.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41806&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Sudan’s crisis deepens with communities trapped in ‘siege conditions’”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/12-11-2025-UNICEF-Sudan-10.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41805</id><title>Air strikes and shelling continue in Gaza</title><updated>2025-12-03T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41805" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/20-11-2025-UNICEF-Gaza-11.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/20-11-2025-UNICEF-Gaza-11.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The UN has called for an investigation into the recent “horrific” killing by Israeli forces of two young brothers in Gaza who were reportedly targeted by a drone while collecting firewood. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41805&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Air strikes and shelling continue in Gaza”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/20-11-2025-UNICEF-Gaza-11.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41804</id><title>World News in Brief: Food insecurity triples, Mozambique aid boost, Uganda elections alert</title><updated>2025-12-03T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41804" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/03-12-2025_FAO_Sudan.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/03-12-2025_FAO_Sudan.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The number of people facing acute food insecurity has tripled since 2016 to nearly 300 million, even as food-sector funding has fallen back to 2016 levels, the Food and Agricultural Organization (FAO) said on Wednesday, launching its first-ever Global Emergency and Resilience Appeal: &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41804&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “World News in Brief: Food insecurity triples, Mozambique aid boost, Uganda elections alert”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/03-12-2025_FAO_Sudan.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41803</id><title>General Assembly LIVE: Resolution adopted demanding Russia return Ukrainian children</title><updated>2025-12-03T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41803" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/17-10-2025-UNICEF-Ukraine-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/17-10-2025-UNICEF-Ukraine-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The UN General Assembly’s emergency special session spotlighting Russian aggression against Ukraine has reconvened in New York where the world body has adopted a resolution demanding Moscow end the forcible transfer and separation from their families, of Ukrainian children. Follow live coverage below and go here for detailed coverage of other key meetings. UN News app users can follow the vote here.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41803&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “General Assembly LIVE: Resolution adopted demanding Russia return Ukrainian children”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/17-10-2025-UNICEF-Ukraine-03.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41802</id><title>Genuine inclusion of persons with disabilities is a win for us all: Guterres</title><updated>2025-12-03T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41802" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/assets/2019/06/11-06-2019-disability-NICA-510023.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/assets/2019/06/11-06-2019-disability-NICA-510023.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Discussions around promoting greater inclusion of people with disabilities in society topped the agenda at the United Nations on Wednesday. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41802&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Genuine inclusion of persons with disabilities is a win for us all: Guterres”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/assets/2019/06/11-06-2019-disability-NICA-510023.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41801</id><title>Myanmar: Opium crop hits 10-year high amid conflict and economic collapse</title><updated>2025-12-03T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41801" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/03-12-2025-UNODC-Myanmar-opium-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/03-12-2025-UNODC-Myanmar-opium-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Opium poppy cultivation in Myanmar has surged to a ten-year high, the United Nations reported on Wednesday, underscoring how prolonged conflict and economic collapse are driving farmers deeper into the illicit economy.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41801&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Myanmar: Opium crop hits 10-year high amid conflict and economic collapse”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/03-12-2025-UNODC-Myanmar-opium-01.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41800</id><title>UN sounds alarm as landmine deaths rise amid funding cuts</title><updated>2025-12-03T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41800" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/14-10-2024_UNDP_Ukraine_landmines-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/14-10-2024_UNDP_Ukraine_landmines-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The deadly legacy of conflicts old and new – from Gaza to Sudan and beyond – continues to kill and maim civilians on a near-daily basis, mine action workers said on Wednesday, as they appealed for greater support for their lifesaving work in the face of deep funding cuts.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41800&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “UN sounds alarm as landmine deaths rise amid funding cuts”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/14-10-2024_UNDP_Ukraine_landmines-02.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41799</id><title>Syria: Hope emerges amid ongoing human rights challenges</title><updated>2025-12-03T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41799" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/02-12-2025_UNICEF_Syria-01.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/02-12-2025_UNICEF_Syria-01.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;For the first time in the country’s history, Syrians are preparing to publicly mark Human Rights Day next week — a small but meaningful step that UN human rights officials say signals a “new chapter” in their engagement with the authorities, and a cautiously optimistic moment for millions seeking change.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2025/12/03/41799&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Syria: Hope emerges amid ongoing human rights challenges”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/02-12-2025_UNICEF_Syria-01.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /></entry></feed><!-- 0.0013s -->